WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.683
Across many machines, one picture governs every trade-off.

00:00:05.683 --> 00:00:09.365
The CAP theorem: three properties, consistency, availability, partition tolerance.

00:00:09.365 --> 00:00:13.048
Here is the catch.

00:00:13.048 --> 00:00:16.731
The cable linking your US and EU data centers can actually be cut, and when it is, the two sides cannot talk.

00:00:16.731 --> 00:00:20.413
You cannot just stop serving.

00:00:20.413 --> 00:00:24.096
So partition tolerance is not optional.

00:00:24.096 --> 00:00:27.779
It is a given, the green circle you always live inside.

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:31.462
That leaves exactly one real choice.

00:00:31.462 --> 00:00:35.144
During a partition, consistency or availability.

00:00:35.144 --> 00:00:38.827
You cannot keep both.

00:00:38.827 --> 00:00:43.248
Say you pick consistency, the purple side.

00:00:43.248 --> 00:00:47.669
When a partition hits, the system refuses any request it cannot verify.

00:00:47.669 --> 00:00:52.090
Spotify's US and EU regions cannot talk, so Premium upgrades block on both sides until they reconnect.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:56.511
Nobody gets a wrong answer, but plenty of people get no answer.

00:00:56.511 --> 00:01:00.932
Consistency winning over availability.

00:01:00.932 --> 00:01:04.601
Now the blue side, availability.

00:01:04.601 --> 00:01:08.270
Both regions keep accepting writes, so nobody is blocked.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:11.938
But when the partition heals, the same account may have two versions, and you have to reconcile them.

00:01:11.938 --> 00:01:15.607
Sometimes automatic, sometimes manual, sometimes you lose data.

00:01:15.607 --> 00:01:19.276
Availability winning over consistency.

00:01:19.276 --> 00:01:24.319
CAP is not a limit you avoid.

00:01:24.319 --> 00:01:29.363
It is a choice you make, explicitly, for every distributed system: during a partition, consistency or availability.

00:01:29.363 --> 00:01:34.406
To see which one a given system picked, and why, read its own docs.

00:01:34.406 --> 00:01:39.450
So the machines agree, stay available, and survive splits.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:44.493
Next: how a single query runs across all of them, with hash and sort.
